    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for fried rice with a real cut of pork in it, not the three mystery cubes the takeout box gives you.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003ePork tenderloin goes on sale constantly and freezes perfectly.\u003c/strong\u003e It is lean, it slices thin without a fight, and buying two when the price drops sets up two batches.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCook your rice the day before.\u003c/strong\u003e This is a free upgrade. Cold rice fries properly, hot rice steams, and the difference is the whole texture of the dish.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eFrozen peas beat canned and fresh on every count here.\u003c/strong\u003e Cheaper per ounce, better color, and they go in straight from the bag with no thawing.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eSesame oil goes in at the end, not in the pan.\u003c/strong\u003e It is expensive per bottle and its flavor cooks right off, so a small drizzle at the finish gets you far more out of it."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cook the rice a day ahead if you can, spread it on a sheet pan to cool, and refrigerate it uncovered.",
                    "Slice the pork tenderloin thin against the grain and season it with salt and black pepper.",
                    "Heat some of the vegetable oil in a large skillet or wok over high heat and cook the pork in batches until just done, then set it aside.",
                    "Beat the eggs, scramble them in the same pan until barely set, and set them aside with the pork.",
                    "Add the rest of the oil and cook the diced onion, carrots, garlic, and ginger until the carrots soften.",
                    "Add the cold rice and the frozen peas and stir-fry hard, pressing the rice against the hot pan so it toasts rather than steams.",
                    "Return the pork and egg, pour in the soy sauce, toss everything together, then finish off the heat with the sesame oil and the sliced green onions."
                ],
